WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.480
Insulin never crosses the cell membrane.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:11.080
Yet within minutes, a muscle cell pulls glucose out of the blood and stores it as

3
00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:15.800
glycogen. The signal runs from the receptor to one kinase, called Akt.

4
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.280
Before insulin arrives, Akt drifts in the cytosol, inactive.

5
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:23.060
How does a message from outside reach it?

6
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:27.233
Insulin binds its receptor, which is a tyrosine kinase.

7
00:00:27.673 --> 00:00:31.793
The receptor adds phosphates to its own tyrosines, and then to those of a docking

8
00:00:31.793 --> 00:00:39.313
protein, IRS-1. IRS-1 is held at the membrane beside the receptor, and has a long

9
00:00:39.313 --> 00:00:40.873
tail with many tyrosines.

10
00:00:41.513 --> 00:00:46.653
Once the receptor has phosphorylated that tail, proteins with SH2 domains dock on

11
00:00:46.653 --> 00:00:53.473
it. An SH2 domain binds a phosphorylated tyrosine, and one such protein is PI

12
00:00:53.473 --> 00:00:54.473
3-kinase.

13
00:00:56.016 --> 00:01:00.156
PI 3-kinase now sits at the membrane, where its substrate is.

14
00:01:00.596 --> 00:01:03.976
That substrate is a lipid called PIP2.

15
00:01:04.456 --> 00:01:09.716
The catalytic subunit of PI 3-kinase adds a phosphate to the 3 position of the

16
00:01:09.716 --> 00:01:12.516
lipid. The product is PIP3.

17
00:01:13.376 --> 00:01:15.456
PIP3 is a membrane address.

18
00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:19.030
Akt has a PH domain at its amino end.

19
00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:21.850
This domain binds PIP3.

20
00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:24.030
So Akt leaves the cytosol.

21
00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:26.070
The lipid holds it against the membrane.

22
00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:28.030
Akt is now at the address.

23
00:01:28.883 --> 00:01:35.343
A second kinase, PDK1, has its own PH domain, so the lipid holds it there too.

24
00:01:36.243 --> 00:01:40.563
PDK1 adds a phosphate to a threonine in the activation loop of Akt.

25
00:01:41.103 --> 00:01:44.083
Akt needs a phosphate on that loop to work well.

26
00:01:44.663 --> 00:01:50.623
A separate kinase complex, mTORC2, adds a second phosphate near the tail, on a

27
00:01:50.623 --> 00:01:55.143
serine. The first phosphate does most of the work, the second adds to it, and

28
00:01:55.143 --> 00:01:56.643
together they give full activity.

29
00:01:57.483 --> 00:02:00.303
Why does Akt need a lipid and two phosphates?

30
00:02:00.903 --> 00:02:05.903
Each is a check: Akt is on mainly where PI 3-kinase has made its lipids.

31
00:02:06.363 --> 00:02:09.503
With no insulin, normal Akt stays in the cytosol.

32
00:02:09.883 --> 00:02:15.143
But give Akt a permanent fatty acid anchor, and it sits at the membrane, gains both

33
00:02:15.143 --> 00:02:17.083
phosphates, and is active.

34
00:02:17.783 --> 00:02:25.243
Keep this rule: PIP3 first puts Akt in the right place, and also opens its folded

35
00:02:25.243 --> 00:02:25.663
shape.
